# DREAMengin — Cursor Operating Law
# Read before touching a single file.

## NON-NEGOTIABLE: THE NEW PART IS THE STANDARD

When the owner hands you a new component, module, or piece of logic:
- It is the fixed, final standard.
- You do NOT alter it to suit old callers.
- You reshape the old callers to conform to it.
- You do NOT wrap it in adapters or compatibility shims unless explicitly told to.
- You do NOT preserve old, broken, or superseded code because it exists.

The existing codebase is a chassis. The new part is the spec. Chassis bends to spec.

## THE CAR RULE

New rims that don't fit wheel bolts → you modify the car.
New engine whose mounts don't align → you reshape the engine bay.
The new part does not change. The surroundings do.

## THE ONLY QUESTION YOU NEED TO ASK

"Is there anything in the existing code that absolutely cannot be touched?"

If the owner doesn't flag it — it is fair game. Reshape it.

## OWNER BUG REPORTS ARE GROUND TRUTH

The owner does not write or read code.
They describe what they see on screen — on a real iPhone, in a real browser session.
They are not describing code. They are describing reality.

If the owner says it's broken — it is broken.
Do NOT argue. Do NOT cite passing tests or green CI.
Do NOT say "I don't see a problem."
Static code analysis is NOT runtime truth.
Owner experience IS runtime truth.
Your job: find the break. If you can't, say so. Never say it's fine.

LITERAL INTERPRETATION — ALWAYS:
- "Nothing works after login" = everything post-login is broken. Full scope.
- "Nothing scrolls" = every scrollable surface. Not one component.
- "It's all broken" = entire described surface is broken.
- Never shrink the reported scope. Never guess what they "probably meant."

## HUMAN AI ARMY ESCALATION PROTOCOL

This repo has the human AI army — a runtime audit agent that crawls the app as a real
iPhone Safari user and reports what it actually experiences.

Files:
- .github/scripts/humanai_audit.py  (audit script)
- agents/humanAI.persona.md         (persona spec)
- .github/workflows/humanai-audit.yml (CI trigger)
- agents/humanAI/personas/          (army personas)
- agents/humanAI/orchestrator.md    (master report)

Run the human AI army when:
- Owner reports a bug you can't find in static analysis.
- You are about to say "the code looks fine" — STOP. Run the human AI army first.
- You need to know what the app actually does, not what you think it does.

The human AI army does not defend code. It reports reality. It outranks your static read.

## BANNED PATTERNS

- Adding backward-compatible adapters to protect broken old code
- Treating existing code as more authoritative than explicit instruction
- Partial output / stubs / TODOs
- Hunting for unrelated broken things outside the requested scope
- Shrinking new parts to fit old architecture
- Arguing with a bug report using static analysis or CI results
- Narrowing a bug report scope ("nothing scrolls" means NOTHING scrolls)

## DREAMENGIN RULES

1. One fixed engine (state, I/O, events, security) — never changes.
2. Unique behaviors = swappable rule-sets outside the engine.
3. Rule-sets = constraints + transformations + parameters only. No infrastructure.
4. Engine applies active rule-set to base state → outcome.
5. Change behavior by swapping rule-sets. Never modify the engine.

Output quality: Synchronized. Intuitive. Coherent. Cohesive. Always complete.

## REPO QUICK FACTS

- Stack: Next.js 16+ / React 19 / Supabase / TypeScript
- Package manager: pnpm (ONLY pnpm — never npm or yarn)
- Node: 24
- Source: app/ components/ lib/
- Preflight: pnpm preflight
- 4 pre-existing test failures in dreamdm-bar-interactions.test.ts — leave them alone
